  "reason": "Evidence: Among single-profile RNA encoders, scvi_n200 is the clear best (acc=0.520, F1=0.498), outperforming pca_n2000 (0.485) and scgpt (0.411). Image-only with resnet50 is very weak (0.208), making multimodal fusion with resnet50 risky. We have zero data on multiprofile or aggregation effects. Question: Does moving from single-profile to multiprofile with simple avgpool aggregation improve upon the best single-profile RNA result (scvi_n200)? This directly extends our strongest configuration along the profile/aggregation axis while holding the encoder fixed. Why this is best: It isolates the effect of multiprofile+aggregation on the best-known encoder, uses the simplest aggregation as a conservative baseline, and the outcome will gate whether the entire multiprofile branch (including multimodal multiprofile configs) is worth further budget. Testing multimodal now would confound the image-modality weakness with the profile/aggregation question."
